NEOWAY N706B OPENCPU SDK & FLASHING GUIDE

This guide maps out the file structures of the Neoway SDKs/Tools on your PC, identifies what was missing, and provides a step-by-step walkthrough to get your drivers installed, compile HelloWorld, and flash the module.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. SDK & TOOLS DIRECTORY GUIDE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Here is where all files are located on your system:

- Workspace Code: E:\projects\DevelopmentLevelCode\Neoway_N706B\Code
  Contains your code workspace, including HelloWorld and Blinky projects.
- Active SDK Root: E:\projects\DevelopmentLevelCode\Neoway_N706B\N706B-A07-STD-OE_CN1X_ITRI-009_SDK\N706B-A07-STD-OE_CN1X_ITRI-009_SDK
  The active compiler, headers, and build scripts.
- SDK API Headers: E:\projects\DevelopmentLevelCode\Neoway_N706B\N706B-A07-STD-OE_CN1X_ITRI-009_SDK\N706B-A07-STD-OE_CN1X_ITRI-009_SDK\include
  API header files (nwy_osi_api.h, nwy_log_api.h, etc.).
- Aboot Flashing Tool: E:\projects\Devolapment files\Neoway\N706B-A07-STD-OE_CN1X_ITRI-009.zip\aboot-tools-2024.10.12-win-x64.exe\aboot-tools-2024.10.12-win-x64.exe\aboot-tools-2024.10.12-win-x64
  Contains aboot.exe (graphic flashing utility) and adownload.exe.
- USB Drivers (ASR): E:\projects\Devolapment files\Neoway\N706B-A07-STD-OE_CN1X_ITRI-009.zip\aboot-tools-2024.10.12-win-x64.exe\aboot-tools-2024.10.12-win-x64.exe\aboot-tools-2024.10.12-win-x64\drivers
  Contains DrvInstaller_x64.exe and generic .inf configuration files.
- API Documentation: E:\projects\DevelopmentLevelCode\Neoway\Neoway_OpenCPU_SDK_API_Notes (17)
  Contains 17 PDF guides on various APIs (GPIO, FOTA, OS, MQTT, etc.).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. MISSING COMPONENT: USB DRIVERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Windows detects the hardware (VID_2949 & PID_7401), but lists them as Unknown Devices (Error code 28: Drivers not installed):
- Mobile AT Interface (Multiple ports)
- Mobile Diag Interface (Diagnostics port)
- Mobile MODEM Interface (Modem data port)

Because the drivers are missing, no virtual COM ports are created, which prevents us from flashing.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. STEP-BY-STEP DRIVER SETUP (WINDOWS 10 / 11)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Since the Neoway N706B uses standard CDC-ACM USB endpoints, the most reliable and signature-safe way to install them under Windows 10/11 is to load Microsoft's built-in USB Serial Device driver. 

Follow these steps for EACH of the failed Mobile AT Interface / Mobile Diag Interface / Mobile MODEM Interface items in Device Manager:

1. Open Device Manager (Win + X -> Device Manager).
2. Locate the failed device with the yellow warning triangle (e.g., Mobile AT Interface).
3. Right-click it and choose Update driver.
4. Select Browse my computer for drivers.
5. Select Let me pick from a list of available drivers on my computer.
6. (If prompted with categories) Select Ports (COM & LPT) and click Next.
7. In the manufacturer list, scroll down and select Microsoft.
8. In the model list, select USB Serial Device.
9. Click Next and choose Yes on any warning prompt.
10. The interface will immediately turn into a functional COM port (e.g., USB Serial Device (COM3)).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. STEP-BY-STEP COMPILE & FLASH GUIDE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

*** CRITICAL: SDK VERSION MISMATCH WARNING ***
- Issue: If your board is running a system firmware version (e.g. Version: SDK_1.011.172) that does not match the compiled application's SDK version (Version: SDK_1.011.156 / A07), the board's dynamic linker/OS will fail to load your app.
- Symptoms: The app flashes successfully in Aboot, but upon reboot, AT+NAPPCHECK? returns an empty value (+NAPPCHECK: ) and no debug logs are printed.
- Solution: You must flash the matching base firmware (N706B-A07-STD-OE_CN1X_ITRI-009.zip) first. This downgrades/aligns the module's system/bootloader to version 1.011.156 which matches the SDK.

Prerequisite: Flash the OpenCPU Base Firmware (Do this once)
Since the module might run standard firmware or a different firmware revision, you must first flash the matching OpenCPU base firmware to the board.
1. Run aboot.exe.
2. Load the base firmware package: E:\projects\DevelopmentLevelCode\Neoway_N706B\Code\N706B-A07-STD-OE_CN1X_ITRI-009.zip (approx. 4.69 MB).
3. Send AT$MYDOWNLOAD=1 in your AT Command serial terminal (COM25).
4. Click Start in aboot to flash the base firmware. The board will reboot with the correct base firmware.
5. Verify on COM20 (Debug log) that it prints Version: SDK_1.011.156.

Once the base firmware is updated, follow these steps to build and flash HelloWorld:

Step A: Compile the Code
1. Open a terminal in HelloWorld.
2. Run .\build_HelloWorld.bat.
3. Wait for the compilation to finish. It will output a ZIP file at:
   E:\projects\DevelopmentLevelCode\Neoway_N706B\Code\HelloWorld\release\nwy_open_app.zip

Step B: Launch Aboot Flash Tool
1. Run aboot.exe from your tools folder.
2. Load the ZIP package: Select the compiled nwy_open_app.zip from your HelloWorld release folder.

Step C: Flash the Module
1. Open a serial terminal (e.g. SSCOM) on the AT Command COM Port (COM25).
2. Send the AT command:
   AT$MYDOWNLOAD=1
   This tells the module to reboot into download mode.
3. Click Start in the aboot flashing utility. It will automatically detect the bootloader port and flash the application binary.

Step D: Verify the App & View Logs
1. Once flashing finishes, the module will reboot automatically.
2. In your AT Command serial terminal (COM25), run the following command to check if the OpenCPU app has loaded successfully:
   AT+NAPPCHECK?
   If it returns HelloWorld_V1.0.0, the app is running!
3. Open COM24 (Neoway USB OPEN_CON) at baud 115200 in SSCOM or PuTTY. You will see:
   Hello World from Neoway N706B OpenCPU via COM24! Count: X, GPIO: Y
4. Since we registered a receive callback, you can also send characters to COM24 and they will be echoed back to verify bidirectional communication!
5. The physical general-purpose GPIO 7 (Pin 21, GPIO_07) will toggle its state (on/off) every 1 second, synchronized with the printed logs. Toggling this pin avoids Cellular Processor watchdog reset loops.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. PRODUCT DOCUMENT COMPARISON & HARDWARE INTEGRATION RULES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Based on the official Neoway Hardware User Guides and web documentation, here is the hardware integration and pin mapping comparison for the N706 series:

A. Physical Package Layout (N706B-CN LCC+LGA)
The N706B-CN is an LCC+LGA module containing 109 pads. The pin layout is divided into:
- Outermost Castellated Edge (LCC, Pins 1 to 44):
  * Bottom Border: Pins 1 to 13 (left-to-right)
  * Right Border: Non-sequential order 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 21, 20, 19, 22 (bottom-to-top)
  * Top Border: Pins 35 down to 23 (left-to-right / counter-clockwise)
  * Left Border: Pins 36 to 44 (top-to-bottom)
- Inner Symmetrical Surface Pads (LGA, Pins 45 to 109):
  * Outer Row: Bottom row (45-53, LGA 99/100), Right col (54-58), Top row (59-67, LGA 103/104), Left col (68-72)
  * Inner Row: Bottom row (73-80, LGA 101/106), Top row (81-88, LGA 102/105)
  * Center Grid: Ground plane (89-94) and status lines (107-109)
  * Corners: Top-Left (98), Bottom-Left (97), Top-Right (96), Bottom-Right (95)

B. Series Pinout Mapping Differences
Different versions of the N706 series use different base chipsets (e.g. ASR1601/1602 vs ASR1605S) which shifts specific hardware pin assignments. Ensure your schematic aligns with the correct sub-model:

| Feature / Signal | Standard N706 (ASR1601) | N706-CB / N706-EA (ASR1602) | N706B-CN (ASR1605S) |
|------------------|-------------------------|-----------------------------|---------------------|
| USB_BOOT         | Pin 53                  | Pin 82                      | Pin 82 (GPIO 9)     |
| ADC Channel 1    | Pin 9 (ADC1)            | Pin 96 (ADC1)               | Pin 96 (ADC1)       |
| ADC Channel 2    | Pin 56 (ADC2)           | Pin 9 (ADC0)                | Pin 9 (ADC2)        |
| Wi-Fi Scan / WCN | ANT_WLAN_2G (Pin 6)     | ANT_WIFI_SCAN (Pin 98)      | ANT_WIFI_SCAN (Pin 98) |

C. Critical Hardware & Safety Integration Rules
1. USB_BOOT (Pin 82): This is a critical bootstrap pin.
   * Rule: Must be kept open/floating during normal power-up.
   * Danger: If grounded during boot, it forces the module into USB emergency download (flashing) mode, preventing normal application startup.
2. MAIN_RI (Pin 20): Ring Indicator pin (Cellular Core owned).
   * Rule: Configure ONLY as input on the AP core.
   * Danger: Do NOT drive high/low from the user application code. Doing so causes register conflicts with the Cellular Processor core and triggers watchdog hardware resets.
3. MAIN_RTS (Pin 23): Hardware UART flow control.
   * Rule: Leave open if unused; keep connected only for UART hardware flow control.
   * Danger: Do not repurpose as general-purpose GPIO.
4. Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC1: Pin 96, ADC2: Pin 9):
   * Rule: The detectable voltage range is 0.1 V to 1.2 V.
   * Danger: Exceeding 1.2 V on these pins will damage the SoC ADC registers permanently. Use a resistor divider network if measuring higher voltages.
5. GND Pins (24 pins):
   * Rule: Connect all GND pins directly to the main PCB ground plane with multiple thermal vias to ensure RF signal stability and avoid overheating.

